Why Are Through Glass Vias Important in Advanced Packaging?

Through-glass vias create vertical electrical pathways through glass substrates or interposers. These connections can enable routing between different layers or components in an electronic package.

Glass offers properties such as electrical insulation, dimensional stability and potentially favorable high-frequency performance. These characteristics make glass-based interconnects relevant to selected radio-frequency devices, MEMS, sensors, photonics and advanced packaging architectures.

Commercial adoption depends on via geometry, metallization quality, manufacturing yield, thermal reliability and compatibility with downstream assembly processes.

Advanced Packaging and Chiplet Integration Support Market Growth

The increasing use of chiplets and heterogeneous integration is creating demand for interconnect technologies that can connect different functional components within a compact package.

TGV solutions are being explored for applications where electrical performance, fine-pitch routing and substrate stability are important. As advanced packaging requirements evolve, manufacturers are evaluating glass-based platforms alongside silicon and organic substrate technologies.

The transition from development and qualification to high-volume production will depend on process consistency, reliability and cost competitiveness.

High-Frequency RF and 5G Applications Create Opportunities

High-frequency RF modules can require interconnect structures with carefully controlled electrical characteristics. Glass substrates may offer advantages in selected designs because of their dielectric properties and dimensional stability.

Applications can include RF modules and related high-frequency electronic systems. However, the suitability of TGV technology depends on the complete package design, material stack and performance requirements.

